	<string>$(FLUTTER_BUILD_NUMBER)</string>
	<!-- Flutter's own engine-level deep-link routing defaults to ON when
	     this key is absent (see FlutterSharedApplication.isFlutterDeepLinkingEnabled
	     in the iOS engine — nil Info.plist value reads as enabled). With
	     it on, EVERY incoming Universal Link/custom-scheme URL is ALSO
	     sent straight to the framework as a raw `pushRouteInformation`
	     call (GoRouter's default platform-route handling) — a second,
	     completely independent navigation, alongside the one
	     DeepLinkService/AppRouter's own pendingProfile/pendingInvite
	     mechanism already performs (see deep_links.dart, app.dart's
	     _onProfileLink/_onInvite, app_router.dart's redirect resume
	     logic). The app_links plugin (AppLinksIosPlugin.swift) always
	     returns `false` from its own continueUserActivity/openURL
	     delegate methods — it only OBSERVES the link for its own
	     stream, it never tells the engine "handled" — so this fallback
	     fires for every single deep link regardless. On a cold launch
	     this races harmlessly behind DeepLinkService's own faster
	     pendingProfile-resume redirect; on a WARM/backgrounded resume
	     (app already running, scanned again via the native Camera) it
	     doesn't race behind anything — it independently `.go()`s
	     GoRouter straight to `/u/<username>` (UsernameProfileResolverScreen,
	     "Loading profile…") on top of/instead of whatever
	     DeepLinkService's own push already did, and that screen's own
	     internal hand-off to the real profile is what a Back press was
	     re-triggering, repeatedly. DeepLinkService's own cold/warm
	     handling (app_links' getInitialLink()/uriLinkStream) does NOT
	     depend on this key at all, so disabling Flutter's redundant
	     fallback leaves the app's ONE actual deep-link path unaffected. -->
	<key>FlutterDeepLinkingEnabled</key>
	<false/>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
